WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.500
Different runtimes use different names, but several concepts appear again and again.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.900
Batch size describes how much work the runtime tries to process together.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:12.130
Microbatch size breaks larger work into smaller chunks so the runtime can fit within

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:13.115
memory limits.

00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:17.195
Maximum batch tokens may limit the total number of prompt or generation tokens being

00:00:17.195 --> 00:00:19.165
processed across active requests.

00:00:19.165 --> 00:00:23.300
Maximum concurrent sequences controls how many active conversations or requests can be in

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:23.890
flight.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:27.300
Context length controls how much prompt and history each request can carry.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.800
Thread count affects CPU execution and support work around the model.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.910
GPU layers or GPU offload settings decide how much of the model runs on the GPU instead of

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:36.575
the CPU.

00:00:36.575 --> 00:00:40.095
Parallelism settings may affect how work is split across devices or streams.

00:00:40.095 --> 00:00:42.100
The confusing part is that names vary.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.920
One runtime may expose n_batch and n_ubatch.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.685
Another may talk about max batch tokens, continuous batching, slots, sequences,

00:00:48.685 --> 00:00:49.790
or scheduler limits.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:53.425
Do not assume two settings are identical just because they sound similar.

00:00:53.425 --> 00:00:56.895
Documentation, release notes, and measurement are part of the tuning process.

00:00:56.895 --> 00:01:01.495
A useful mental model is to ask what each setting controls: memory footprint, concurrency,

00:01:01.495 --> 00:01:06.000
prompt processing, decode scheduling, CPU support work, or GPU utilization.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.225
Once you know what kind of constraint a setting touches,

00:01:08.225 --> 00:01:11.085
you can make smaller changes and interpret the result more confidently.

00:01:11.085 --> 00:01:14.155
The setting name matters less than understanding the behavior it changes.

00:01:14.155 --> 00:01:18.100
When a runtime exposes many knobs, resist the urge to turn all of them at once.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.140
Start with the settings that define the workload boundary: context length, concurrency,

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.950
and maximum prompt or batch tokens.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:27.195
Then tune the settings that affect how the work is divided internally.

00:01:27.195 --> 00:01:30.890
That order makes failures easier to interpret and rollback easier to manage.
